        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="proj-stage">
        <div class="proj-stage__head">
          <span class="proj-stage__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="proj-stage__label">V1</p>
          <p class="proj-stage__note">custom PCB, new case, finished build</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proj-stage__rows">
          <div class="jrow">
            <ul class="jrow__half">
              <li class="proj-stage__item" style="--a: 0.75">
                <figure>
                  <div class="proj-stage__frame">
                    <img src="/images/4square/v1-pcb.jpg" alt="4square V1 with the back off, showing the custom PCB" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" />
                  </div>
                </figure>
              </li>
              <li class="proj-stage__item" style="--a: 0.75">
                <figure>
                  <div class="proj-stage__frame">
                    <img src="/images/4square/v1-front.jpg" alt="4square V1 finished, clock face" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" />
                  </div>
                </figure>
              </li>
              <li class="proj-stage__item" style="--a: 0.75">
                <figure>
                  <div class="proj-stage__frame">
                    <img src="/images/4square/v1-tickers.jpg" alt="4square V1 finished, showing a four-panel page" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" />
                  </div>
                </figure>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="jrow">
            <ul class="jrow__half">
              <li class="proj-stage__item" style="--a: 1.33333">
                <figure>
                  <div class="proj-stage__frame">
                    <img src="/images/4square/v1-early-front.jpg" alt="4square V1, front, showing the clock face" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" />
                  </div>
                </figure>
              </li>
              <li class="proj-stage__item" style="--a: 1.33333">
                <figure>
                  <div class="proj-stage__frame">
                    <img src="/images/4square/v1-early-desk.jpg" alt="4square V1 on the desk" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" />
                  </div>
                </figure>
              </li>
              <li class="proj-stage__item" style="--a: 1.03394">
                <figure>
                  <div class="proj-stage__frame">
                    <img src="/images/4square/v1-dark.jpg" alt="4square in the dark, OLEDs glowing" width="792" height="766" loading="lazy" />
                  </div>
                </figure>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr class="proj-divider" />

  <!-- ── custom PCB ───────────────────── -->
  <section class="proj-section" id="pcb" data-crumb="pcb">
    <p class="proj-section-label"><Icon name="chip" class="proj-section-label__icon" />custom pcb</p>
    <h2 class="proj-section-heading">replacing the jumper wires with one board</h2>
    <p>
      The prototype and V0 were four screen modules, a mux breakout and an ESP32 joined by
      jumper wires and protoboard. It worked, but the wiring was fragile, it filled the case, and every rebuild meant rewiring.
      I replaced all of it with one carrier board. The four screens plug into the front, everything
      else sits on the back, and the board matches the inside of the case at 74&times;74 mm, which
      keeps the walls 2 mm thick all round.
    </p>
    <p>
      The board is generated from code: the schematic and placement are scripts, KiCad checks the
      result, and an autorouter lays the copper, so every revision rebuilds from scratch.
      It went through revisions A to N over about a week in July. Rev C was the first square
      layout, rev D moved the power chain onto a centre spine, rev E added the four buttons,
      rev F settled on 74&times;74 mm with even margins around the screens, rev G turned the
      back into a &ldquo;kit&rdquo; you look at through the clear back, with axial resistors
      laid out as a labelled ladder, rev I added the DS3231 clock, the temperature sensor and
      the light sensor, and rev L moved to cheaper LEDs and labelled every part. An audit of rev L found blockers, and rev N fixed all of them: it passes DRC at
      JLCPCB&rsquo;s manufacturing limits with 74 footprints on two layers, and JLCPCB assembles the
      surface-mount parts. The first batch was ordered from that design as rev O.
    </p>
    <div class="proj-gallery proj-gallery--row">
      <figure class="proj-gallery__item" style="flex: 1">
        <img src="/images/4square/pcb-front-full.webp" alt="KiCad render of the front: the four OLED sockets on the 74 x 74 mm board" width="1768" height="1768" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="proj-stage__cap">KiCad render of the front &middot; the four OLED sockets</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="proj-gallery__item" style="flex: 1">
        <img src="/images/4square/pcb-back-full.webp" alt="KiCad render of the whole back of the board, edges and corner mounting holes included: resistor ladder, status LEDs, mux, RTC, EEPROM socket, coin cell and the ESP32-C3 module" width="1800" height="1800" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="proj-stage__cap">KiCad render of the back &middot; the resistor ladder, LEDs, mux, RTC and EEPROM</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-widget-list">
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">ESP32-C3</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">SuperMini module, its own USB-C on the bottom edge</span>
    </li>
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">Screens</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">four OLED sockets behind an I&sup2;C mux</span>
    </li>
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">DS3231</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">real-time clock with a coin-cell backup</span>
    </li>
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">SHT31</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">temperature and humidity, under the vent</span>
    </li>
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">Light</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">phototransistor for auto-dimming</span>
    </li>
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">24LC256</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">EEPROM for settings, in a socket</span>
    </li>
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">LEDs</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">three RGB status LEDs</span>
    </li>
    <li class="proj-widget-item">
      <span class="proj-widget-name">Buttons</span>
      <span class="proj-widget-desc">MODE, SET, UP, DOWN on the side edges</span>
    </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr class="proj-divider" />

  <!-- ── case CAD ─────────────────────── -->
  <section class="proj-section" id="case" data-crumb="case">
    <p class="proj-section-label"><Icon name="wrench" class="proj-section-label__icon" />case</p>
    <h2 class="proj-section-heading">case CAD in build123d</h2>
    <p>
      The V1 case is code CAD: every part is a build123d Python script, so a change to the board becomes a changed number and a rebuild instead of an afternoon of
      redrawing.
      The printed set is rev 5, three PETG parts: a front shell that holds the four screens
      just below the face, and a retainer split into two halves that clamps the screens from
      behind. The back is rev 6, a 3 mm laser-cut acrylic panel that keeps the board visible,
      with two finger notches, a slotted vent over the temperature sensor and a printed
      standoff frame behind it. Four M3&times;16 self-tapping screws hold the whole stack together.
    </p>
    <div class="proj-gallery proj-gallery--row">
      <figure class="proj-gallery__item" style="flex: 1.30588">
        <img src="/images/4square/4square-exploded-still.webp" alt="Exploded render of the case: screws, acrylic back, standoff frame, PCB, retainer, screens and shell" width="888" height="680" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="proj-stage__cap">exploded still</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="proj-gallery__item" style="flex: 1.33333">
        <img src="/images/4square/case-screens-on.webp" alt="The assembled clock with the four screens lit showing the clock face" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="proj-stage__cap">assembled &middot; screens lit with the clock face</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="proj-gallery__item" style="flex: 1.33333">
        <img src="/images/4square/case-acrylic-engraving.webp" alt="Render of the rev 6 acrylic back with the engraved text and vent slots" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="proj-stage__cap">rev 6 acrylic back &middot; engraved on the inner face</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      The engraving reads &ldquo;SAWYER&rdquo; and &ldquo;SMITH&rdquo; on either side of the
      USB-C opening, with &ldquo;4SQUARE V1&rdquo; in the middle, in newstroke, the same font
      as the board&rsquo;s own silkscreen, pulled straight out of KiCad. It is engraved on the
      inner face so the surface you touch stays smooth. A laser marks whichever face is up, so the panel is cut inner face up and the DXF is drawn
      mirrored to match: in the file the text reads backwards and the vent sits top left. Once the
      panel is turned over and assembled, the text reads normally from outside.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="proj-divider" />

  <!-- ── firmware ─────────────────────── -->
  <section class="proj-section" id="firmware" data-crumb="firmware">
    <p class="proj-section-label"><Icon name="chip" class="proj-section-label__icon" />firmware</p>
    <h2 class="proj-section-heading">buttons, pages and burn-in protection</h2>
    <p>
      There is no menu. Each button owns a page, and pressing it again cycles that page. A long
      press on MODE toggles the status LEDs, and a long press on SET opens the one settings page:
      light sensitivity, temperature unit, and a daily screens-off window. Settings live in a
      wear-levelled ring on the EEPROM, so they survive a power cut.
    </p>
    <ul class="proj-widget-list">
      <li class="proj-widget-item">
        <span class="proj-widget-name">MODE &middot; clock</span>
        <span class="proj-widget-desc">hour, minute, weekday, date in several digit styles</span>
      </li>
      <li class="proj-widget-item">
        <span class="proj-widget-name">SET &middot; readings</span>
        <span class="proj-widget-desc">temperature, humidity, light, uptime</span>
      </li>
      <li class="proj-widget-item">
        <span class="proj-widget-name">UP &middot; stopwatch</span>
        <span class="proj-widget-desc">start or resume from any page</span>
      </li>
      <li class="proj-widget-item">
        <span class="proj-widget-name">DOWN &middot; animations</span>
        <span class="proj-widget-desc">48&times;48 animations at 8 fps</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The stopwatch is the newest face; it replaced the stock ticker in October. It shows minutes top left
      and seconds top right in the clock&rsquo;s outline digits, the state bottom left, hours
      bottom right. UP starts or resumes it from anywhere, DOWN on it pauses and then clears, and
      it keeps counting while you look at other pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      Burn-in is the main way a clock on OLEDs fails, so the protection is built into the drawing
      path instead of left to each face. Drawing code only gets a canvas in RAM. One function copies
      that canvas to the panel, and on the way it shifts the image along a path that visits every
      offset evenly and applies a brightness cap; the light sensor dims it further at night. Because
      no face can write to a panel directly, every face gets the shift and the cap. The second
      safeguard is a layout prover that runs on my laptop: it compiles the same drawing code
      that ships and checks every screen the firmware can draw for overlapping elements, ink
      outside the safe area and the lit-pixel budget. If any check fails, the flash script refuses to flash.
    </p>
    <div class="proj-callout">
      The same source builds two ways. The demo build has no radio code at all and keeps time from the clock chip; it is the version I show people. The full build adds WiFi and over-the-air
      updates for running it at home.
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="proj-divider" />

  <!-- ── source ───────────────────────── -->
  <section class="proj-section" id="source" data-crumb="source">
    <p class="proj-section-label"><Icon name="rocket" class="proj-section-label__icon" />source</p>
    <div class="proj-cta">
      <p class="proj-cta-heading">firmware, demo image and build notes on GitHub</p>
      <p>
        The repo has the firmware source, a prebuilt demo image you can flash with one command,
        the layout prover, and hardware notes with the board summary and pin map. It is MIT
        licensed.
      </p>
      <a
        class="proj-cta-link"
        href="https://github.com/partypancake8/4square_production"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
      >
        github.com/partypancake8/4square_production &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
